(i) The power is negative, so the lens is a concave (diverging) lens.

\(f = \dfrac{1}{P} = \dfrac{1}{-2{\cdot}5} = -0{\cdot}4\) m \(= -40\) cm.

A concave lens is prescribed to correct myopia (short-sightedness).

(ii) A magnification of \(-2\) for a lens means:

(I) the image is real and inverted (negative m);

(II) the image is twice the size of the object (magnified);

(III) since \(m = \dfrac{v}{u}\), \(v = -2 \times (-20) = +40\) cm: the image is 40 cm from the lens on the other side, beyond 2F (the lens is convex, of focal length about 13.3 cm);

(IV) the height of the image is negative.

(iii) Lens A, of focal length 10 cm, shows the higher degree of convergence or divergence. Power is \(P = \dfrac{1}{f}\), so the shorter the focal length, the greater the power to converge or diverge light.
